      {
        "id": 70549,
        "name": "arenson9",
        "username": "arenson9",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-08-12T18:07:49.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p><span>I think the order is:</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>1) Damage is declared, which constitutes determining the original amount, type, source, and target of the damage.</p><p>3) Damage \"is dealt\" by the source, at which point one or more of the 2 redirect effects might modify the target.</p><div>-- Steps 1 and 3 are the same thing</div><div>&nbsp;</div><p>2) Damage increases/reductions that affect damage dealt by a source are applied.</p><p>6) Damage increases/reductions that affect damage dealt to a target are applied.</p><div>-- Steps 2 and 6 are the same thing. Also in this section is anything that changes the type of the damage. All of the increases, decreases, and type changes are considered in card-played order, but because the amount can't affect the type, it's easier to do the type first so as not to have to go back and redo the amounts.</div><div>&nbsp;</div><p><span>4) Damage \"would be dealt\" to the target, at which point one or more of the 8 redirect effects might modify the target.</span></p><p>-- And if redirection occurs, one needs to return to the previous step of considering increases, reductions, and types, except that type is never based on target (to date), so that can effectively be skipped.</p><p><span>5) Immunity may cancel the damage entirely, ending the sequence immediately.</span></p><p><span>7) The target's hp is reduced.</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><span>I'm sorry I don't have references for them, but my memory is that the designers have clearly stated that immunity happens very last, after reductions, and that after a redirection, all increases and decreases are recalculated based on the new target.&nbsp;</span></p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T02:49:26.986Z",
